  <data name="Text_WslDnsTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Repair hotspot DNS (WSL)</value></data>
  <data name="Text_WslDnsDescription" xml:space="preserve"><value>Advanced recovery for a hotspot that connects but cannot resolve websites when WSL is installed. Recreates only the identified WSL virtual network, then restores WSL. Requires confirmation.</value></data>
  <data name="Text_WslDnsConfirmation" xml:space="preserve"><value>This will interrupt the hotspot, WSL distributions, containers and sites hosted in WSL. Save your work first. The app will recreate the identified WSL network, start the hotspot, restart previously running distributions and update existing IPv4 port forwards that still point to the old WSL address. The IP may change; applications and other integrations may need manual attention. A recovery journal and logs will be saved beside the executable. This does not delete distributions or their files. Allow this recovery now?</value></data>
  <data name="Text_WslDnsConfirmButton" xml:space="preserve"><value>Repair and restart WSL</value></data>
  <data name="Text_WslDnsCompleted" xml:space="preserve"><value>Recovery completed and WSL restoration was verified. Reconnect your phone using DHCP and test a new website with mobile data off. Check your WSL applications too. This does not yet confirm client internet access. Logs: {0}</value></data>
  <data name="Text_WslDnsFailed" xml:space="preserve"><value>Recovery or restoration was not completed. Check dns-recovery.log and dns-recovery-state.json in {0}. Running this action again resumes pending restoration before any new removal.</value></data>
  <data name="Text_WslDnsTimeout" xml:space="preserve"><value>The worker is taking longer than expected and may still be restoring WSL. It was not terminated. Do not start another repair. Check the logs in {0}.</value></data>
